  "content": {
    "id": "p43-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "Find $a$, if the normal to $y=\\sqrt{x+a}$ at the point where $x=4$ has gradient $-6$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=5$",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y&=\\sqrt{x+a}=(x+a)^{\\frac12}\\\\\\frac{dy}{dx}&=\\frac12(x+a)^{-\\frac12}(1)\\\\\\frac{dy}{dx}&=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+a}}\\end{align*}$$\nAt $x=4$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{dy}{dx}&=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4+a}}\\end{align*}$$\nThe normal gradient is the negative reciprocal of the tangent gradient.\n$$\\begin{align*}-6&=-1\\div\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4+a}}\\\\-6&=-2\\sqrt{4+a}\\\\3&=\\sqrt{4+a}\\\\9&=4+a\\\\a&=5\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
